{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a certificate authority named Fleet Internal CA on node1 and install it as a trusted system CA on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 created a self-issued CA whose subject and issuer are CN=Fleet Internal CA, installed it in the system anchor directory, and successfully verified the issued node2 certificate using default system trust, including after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b4034a78b1314dd1","cmd-73c533d718ea4c7a","cmd-ca91719296954656"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Issue node2 a TLS server certificate from Fleet Internal CA.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed certificate has subject CN=node2, issuer Fleet Internal CA, server-auth usage, and SANs for DNS:node2 and IP:10.100.196.11. Certificate-chain and hostname verification both succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-63494cc7ffdf4533","cmd-93daf9efc239408a","cmd-d27c0ba8e58f476e","cmd-294dad1967d54097"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Install Fleet Internal CA as a trusted system certificate authority on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2's final anchor is parseable and has the same SHA-256 fingerprint as node1's CA. Default system verification of the server certificate succeeded before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cfca43fb325a4b15","cmd-b965210c04444927","cmd-294dad1967d54097"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Configure node2 to serve trusted HTTPS on TCP port 8443.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 had an enabled, active service listening on 0.0.0.0:8443, with the port allowed persistently through firewalld. Trusted local and remote TLS hostname verification returned success and HTTPS returned the expected content. A bounded node2 restart showed a changed boot ID followed by recovery of the service, listener, firewall rule, certificate trust, and content; the final lifecycle snapshot independently observed the listener with no failed services.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6a95545ef9f74841","cmd-c26f60c813aa4aef","cmd-b965210c04444927","cmd-7fb696c628024c14","cmd-269dcc730b904733","cmd-631afafe5c91482e","cmd-294dad1967d54097","cmd-486d983e6f7d46e2","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64. OpenSSL was installed from the platform's RHEL 10 repositories, and post-execution package-health checks succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ea2bcb7da5d44711","cmd-286b75e6d3bd47ee","cmd-44171e667929498d","cmd-77b2ae8f8b5b425d","cmd-294dad1967d54097","cmd-486d983e6f7d46e2","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"The resulting services and package state were healthy, and the required port exposure was limited to TCP/8443. Two hygiene issues are evident. First, the initial node2 installation wrote a malformed CA anchor and refreshed trust; this was a reverted, low-risk failed mutation later corrected by replacing the anchor with the valid CA (cmd-f641854a542b4779, cmd-73f95a775d704684, cmd-cfca43fb325a4b15). Second, temporary root-level transfer credentials were retained: the cluster public key was appended to root's authorized_keys on node1, and the cluster private key was installed on node2 without subsequent cleanup. Although used to repair the CA transfer, leaving both credentials behind was unnecessary residue and expands privileged access (cmd-490081b4e16f482c, cmd-db454d06ba39488c). No failed services, package damage, or unrelated temporary-file residue was observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f641854a542b4779","cmd-73f95a775d704684","cmd-cfca43fb325a4b15","cmd-490081b4e16f482c","cmd-db454d06ba39488c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, an acknowledged restart, a different post-restart boot identity, and recovered task state. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate both restarts. The principal concern is operational hygiene from retained root SSH transfer credentials, not task functionality.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /root/.ssh, so the terminal trace proves creation of the transfer credentials but does not independently confirm their final contents; no cleanup command is present.","The HTTPS implementation uses a custom Python service running as root. Its systemd sandboxing is shown, but the captured evidence does not provide a broader security assessment of that implementation."]}
